        text: 'We\'re used to thinking of parasites as something disgusting: viruses, worms, bacteria living off others. But parasitism permeates the entire architecture of reality.',
        fullText: 'We\'re used to thinking of parasites as something disgusting: viruses, worms, bacteria living off others. But parasitism permeates the entire architecture of reality.\n\nThe universe erases differences. Hot things cool down, cold things warm up, gradients equalize, entropy grows. While energy flows between the hot Sun and cold space, strange temporary patterns can emerge on this current.\n\nThey get in the way of energy and live off its movement. Physicists call them dissipative structures. A convection cell in a pot of soup. A hurricane over a warm ocean. A candle flame. A living cell. All of them persist only as long as a flow passes through them: remove the heat — convection disappears, remove the warm ocean — the hurricane dies, remove metabolism — the cell ceases to be a cell.\n\nThe second law of thermodynamics is not violated. Local order arises precisely because overall disorder grows even faster: the structure sustains itself by helping the universe dissipate energy more efficiently.\n\nBut then it gets really interesting.\n\nSome structures begin to copy the way they persist in the current. Copying is imperfect, errors are inevitable, and where there are copies, variations, and selection — Darwinian logic kicks in: some ways of persisting turn out to be more stable than others.\n\nThis gives rise to a pyramid of parasitism. Each subsequent level doesn\'t build an energy machine from scratch — it steals it from the previous level. A living cell extracts energy itself: it builds metabolism, catches sunlight, breaks down molecules, uses chemical gradients — it is directly embedded in the physical current of the world.\n\nA virus can no longer do this. It has no complete metabolism, no energy economy of its own. It comes to a ready-made cell and forces it to print new copies of itself. It steals an already assembled energy-extraction system.\n\nNext comes an even more abstract parasite — an idea. It has no body, it doesn\'t breathe, doesn\'t synthesize proteins, doesn\'t break down sugar, and doesn\'t exist at all outside a carrier. But it knows how to do the main thing: get into a brain, stay there, and make that brain transmit it further. An idea steals the resource that cellular metabolism built many levels higher — human attention. And attention is expensive: the brain consumes about 20% of all the body\'s energy, though it weighs only 2%.\n\nThe result is a ladder: from an energy gradient to metabolism, from metabolism to viral replication, from viral replication to ideas, memes, religions, ideologies, and Telegram posts. First organisms compete for food, then viruses compete for cells, then ideas compete for minds.\n\nThe higher the level, the less it resembles material life and the more it resembles a ghost. But an idea\'s reality is easy to verify: it changes the behavior of its carrier. A person spends time on it, defends it, repeats it, argues for it, sometimes kills for it, sometimes dies for it.\n\nSo a thought is a replicator that uses our head as a habitat. And the most successful parasite in the universe is the one you\'re thinking about right now.'
